THE OOM ROOT CAUSE (singleplayer terrain demo 'physics frozen, RAM climbs to ~4GB OOM') - found via DevTools CPU profile of the OOM run, NOT the earlier guesses (textures/snapshot-backlog/veg-shader all wrong). The profile: Octree.js = 47893/119586 samples (~40% CPU, dwarfing idle+everything); UpdateCounters jsHeapSizeUsed hit 3894MB while DOM nodes stayed flat at 527 and listeners 184 -> NOT DOM/GPU/textures, it is the WORKER's src/spatial/Octree.js SpatialIndex backed by d3-octree. d3-octree DEGENERATES CATASTROPHICALLY on (a) NaN/Infinity coords and (b) two DISTINCT points closer than float epsilon - it recurses to separate the pair and never terminates, allocating tree nodes to ~4GB while pinning a worker core; the pinned core starves the tick = the 'physics frozen' symptom, and the worker runs in the page process so its 4GB = page OOM. A transient bad/near-duplicate entity or player position on the planet heightfield trips it. FIX (commit a4ca9f40/8cc6e33c) in SpatialIndex.insert (update() routes through insert): drop non-finite coords via Number.isFinite, and QUANTIZE every coord to 1cm (Math.round(x*100)/100) so near-coincident distinct points collapse to EXACTLY equal -> d3-octree stores them as a chained leaf (data.next) instead of subdividing forever. 1cm is far finer than the relevance query radius. DIAGNOSTIC LESSON: when JS heap climbs to OOM but DOM nodes + listeners are FLAT, it is a pure JS allocation - pull the trace's CPU PROFILE (cpuProfile.nodes + samples -> self-hit ranking) to name the hot function; here one library (d3-octree) was 40% of CPU and the obvious culprit. The user-driven in-page bisect (?veg=none etc.) + their reading of the call tree (createTerrainPanel/appendChild/_buildWorldScenery as the deepest GC frame, heap 1.1mb-4g, major GC 5.4s) pointed the way; appendChild/createTerrainPanel were red herrings on the stack, the d3-octree allocation under _buildWorldScenery's tick was the real one. Supersedes [[singleplayer-snapshot-postmessage-oom-leak]] and [[gpu-program-leak-broken-veg-on-planet-fallback]] as THE main OOM (those were real but secondary fixes).